  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued below normal temperatures the rest of the \nweekend with wet snow and rain showers slowly winding down tonight \nand Sunday.\n\nA mid/upper level trough axis and an accompanying area of deeper \nsynoptic moisture will result in mainly light precipitation \nincreasing in coverage and becoming at least somewhat more steady \nthis afternoon into early this evening...with this aided by moist \nisentropic ascent/DCVA out ahead of the trough axis. Through late \nthis afternoon...marginal thermal profiles (which will actually be a \nbit warmer compared to Friday due to weak warm advection in the \nlower levels out ahead of the trough) will support mainly rain \nacross the lower elevations...with more in the way of wet snow \n(possibly mixed with some rain at times) across the higher \nelevations. Late this afternoon through tonight...the combination of \nnocturnal cooling and weak low-level cold advection will then drive \na changeover back to primarily wet snow...with the precipitation \nalso becoming more scattered and devolving back into more localized \nareas of orographic and lake-induced pcpn following the departure of \nthe trough axis and deeper synoptic moisture. Additional snow accums \nthrough tonight will range from little to nothing across the lower \nelevations to an inch or two across the higher terrain.\n\nSunday and Sunday night ridging over the Upper Midwest will \ngradually build eastward and into our region...with accompanying \nsubsidence resulting in a gradual lowering of inversion heights and \na commensurate drying out of the prime dendritic snow growth zone \naloft. This will result in lingering scattered orographic and lake-\neffect wet snow and/or rain showers south and southeast of the lakes \ngradually winding down from west to east Sunday and evening...with \ndry weather then expected for the balance of Sunday night. Any \nadditional snow accumulations through this time will be on the order \nof a half inch or less and mainly confined to the higher terrain... \nwith mostly cloudy conditions and somewhat below normal temps \notherwise continuing. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by Monday, with temperatures \ntrending notably warmer by midweek.\n\nExpansive/strengthening ridging at the surface and aloft will \ncontinue to build from the Great Plains and across the Great Lakes \nand Ohio Valley right through the middle of next week...before \nslowly flattening late in the week. This will bring a return to fair \ndry weather that currently looks to last through at least \nThursday...along with partly to mostly sunny days Tuesday through \nThursday. Temperature-wise...readings will initially remain a bit \nbelow normal on Monday...before rebounding nicely through midweek as \nthe axis of the ridge slides east of our longitude and allows a \nwarmer southerly to southwesterly flow to develop across our area. \nThis will result in temperatures climbing back to notably above \nnormal levels by Wednesday and especially Thursday...when daytime \nhighs look to range from the mid 50s to lower 60s in most places.",
